// h1read.nv — the load-bearing interface: an HTTP/1.1 reader that
// performs nothing.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// THE SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS THIS ONE
//
// `http-codec-nv` is a `core` package, so its budget is no effects at
// all: it may not open a socket, set a timeout, or wait.  An HTTP
// message is exactly the thing a caller wants to stream, and a large
// one is exactly the thing a caller cannot afford to hold.  Those facts
// are reconciled by a FEED-AND-DRAIN STATE MACHINE: the host holds the
// connection, hands this package whatever bytes it has, and takes back
// the events those bytes completed.
//
//     var r = h1read.reader(ReadsRequests, h1read.default_limits())
//     let step = h1read.feed(r, chunk)!      // as many times as you like
//     let more = h1read.take(step.reader)!   // until `event` is None
//
// ONE EVENT PER CALL, and `event: None` means "not yet" rather than
// "never".  That is the same distinction `hci-codec-nv` draws between
// its `Scan` and its `DecodeError`, arrived at here for the same
// reason: a partial message is the normal case on a socket, and a codec
// that reported it as an error would have every caller filtering one
// error variant out of its logs forever.
//
// WHY A READER READS ONE DIRECTION
//
// `H1Role` fixes what a reader parses, because a request head and a
// response head are two different grammars that begin with tokens you
// cannot tell apart until the second field.  A server holds a
// `ReadsRequests`; a client holds a `ReadsResponses`; nothing holds
// one that guesses.
//
// THE ONE THING A CLIENT MUST TELL THE READER
//
// A response's framing depends on the method of the request it answers
// (`h1msg.response_framing` says why), and a reader cannot know that:
// the request went out through a socket this package never saw.  So a
// client calls `expect` with the method it sent, before the response
// arrives.  A `ReadsResponses` reader that was never told refuses with
// `MessageStillOpen` rather than guessing GET — guessing GET is how a
// client hangs forever on a HEAD.

// H1Role is which direction of the conversation a reader parses.
pub enum H1Role
    // A server's reader: request heads in, response heads out.
    ReadsRequests
    // A client's reader: response heads in, and `expect` must be told
    // the method of each request that went out.
    ReadsResponses

// H1Limits is what a reader will refuse to grow past.
//
// Every one of these exists because the corresponding unbounded version
// is a denial of service that costs the attacker one open socket: a
// head with no blank line, a header section of a million fields, a
// chunk claiming 2^63 bytes.  They are a value the caller owns rather
// than constants this package chose, because an embedded consumer wants
// numbers three orders of magnitude smaller than a reverse proxy does.
pub struct H1Limits
    // Bytes in the start line and header section together, before the
    // blank line that ends them.  Exceeding it is `HeadTooLarge`, which
    // `h1err.status_for` answers 431.
    max_head: Int
    // Fields in one header section, and separately in one trailer
    // section.  Exceeding it is `TooManyHeaders`.
    max_headers: Int
    // Bytes in one chunk of a chunked body.  Exceeding it is
    // `ChunkTooLarge`.  It bounds the buffer, not the message: a body
    // of any size passes through in chunks this big.
    max_chunk: Int

// H1Event is one thing that came out of a reader.
//
// A message arrives as a head, then zero or more `BodyBytes`, then at
// most one `Trailers`, then exactly one `MessageDone`.  A caller that
// only wants whole small messages accumulates the `BodyBytes` itself;
// a caller streaming a 4 GB upload writes each one out and keeps
// nothing, which is the case the head/body split exists for.
pub enum H1Event
    // A request head, complete.  Only from a `ReadsRequests` reader.
    RequestHead(req: H1Request)
    // A response head, complete.  Only from a `ReadsResponses` reader.
    // A 1xx arrives as its own `ResponseHead` followed immediately by
    // `MessageDone`, and the real response follows — `h1msg`'s
    // `is_informational` is how a caller tells them apart.
    ResponseHead(resp: H1Response)
    // Some of the body.  NOT a whole chunk and not a whole anything:
    // the reader hands over what it has, chunk framing already removed,
    // and the number of these events for a given body is an artefact of
    // how the host read its socket.  A caller that treats one
    // `BodyBytes` as one application message has written a bug that
    // works on localhost.
    BodyBytes(data: [u8])
    // The trailer section of a chunked body.  At most one, immediately
    // before `MessageDone`, and absent when there was none.
    Trailers(fields: H1Headers)
    // The message ended.  After this the reader is ready for the next
    // one on the same connection — if `h1msg.may_reuse` said there may
    // be a next one.
    MessageDone

// H1Step is a reader and the one event that came out of it, if one did.
//
// A struct rather than a tuple because a tuple's components come out
// only through `match`, which reads badly for a value whose two halves
// are used at different places: the caller reassigns one and dispatches
// on the other.  csv-nv returns a tuple here and hci-codec-nv a struct;
// this package follows hci-codec-nv, because an HTTP caller loops on
// the event and carries the reader across iterations rather than
// destructuring both once.
pub struct H1Step
    reader: H1Reader
    // `None` means "no complete event in what I have been given yet",
    // which on a socket is the ordinary case and not a fault.
    event: ?H1Event

// H1Reader is the state between "some bytes turned up" and "here is an
// event".
//
// Opaque: the fields are here because a struct needs them, and a caller
// that reaches into one has coupled itself to a buffering decision this
// package intends to change.  `pending_len` and `offset` are the two
// facts about a reader that are anybody's business.
pub struct H1Reader
    role: H1Role
    limits: H1Limits
    pending: [u8]
    consumed: Int

// H1Drained is a reader and every event a drain produced.
pub struct H1Drained
    reader: H1Reader
    events: [H1Event]

// default_limits is a head of 64 KiB, 128 fields and chunks of 1 MiB.
//
// The numbers are nginx's `large_client_header_buffers` and Go's
// `MaxHeaderBytes` rounded to the same order, which is to say: what a
// server on the open internet survives with.  An embedded caller should
// pass its own, an order of magnitude smaller.
pub fn default_limits() -> H1Limits
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.default_limits")

// embedded_limits is a head of 1 KiB, 16 fields and chunks of 512
// bytes.
//
// Named rather than left to each device caller to invent, because the
// number that matters on a microcontroller is the one that fits beside
// everything else in 64 KiB of RAM, and every caller inventing it
// separately gets a different answer.
pub fn embedded_limits() -> H1Limits
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.embedded_limits")

// reader is a reader holding nothing, for one direction and one set of
// limits.
pub fn reader(role: H1Role, limits: H1Limits) -> H1Reader
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.reader")

// expect tells a client's reader the method of a request that has gone
// out, so the response to it can be framed.
//
// Called once per request, in the order the requests were written —
// which is the order the responses come back in, because HTTP/1.1
// pipelining is the only ordering there is.  A `ReadsRequests` reader
// ignores this.
//
// It is a function on the reader and not a field on `feed` because a
// pipelining client sends three requests before the first response
// arrives, and the queue of methods has to live somewhere the reader
// can reach.
pub fn expect(r: H1Reader, sent: H1Method) -> H1Reader
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.expect")

// feed adds bytes and takes one event, if the addition completed one.
//
// Drains ONE event.  A feed that completes three — a small request that
// arrived in a single packet completes its head, its body and its end —
// leaves two inside, and `take` is what gets them.  `drain` is that
// loop written once.
//
// ```novo
// use h1read
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let r = h1read.reader(ReadsRequests, h1read.default_limits())
//     match h1read.feed(r, [0x47 as u8, 0x45 as u8, 0x54 as u8])
//         Ok(step) =>
//             match step.event
//                 None    => println("partial, as expected")
//                 Some(_) => println("an event")
//         Err(e)   => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn feed(r: H1Reader, bytes: [u8]) -> Result<H1Step, H1DecodeError>
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.feed")

// take takes one event if a complete one is already buffered, adding
// nothing.
//
// `event` is `None` when the buffer holds a prefix, and the reader
// comes back unchanged.  A caller loops on this until `None` and then
// goes back to its socket.
pub fn take(r: H1Reader) -> Result<H1Step, H1DecodeError>
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.take")

// finish tells the reader the peer closed, and takes the last event.
//
// This is not a formality.  A response framed `UntilClose` ENDS at the
// close — the close is the framing — so its `MessageDone` exists only
// if somebody says the close happened.  A close in the middle of any
// other message is `MessageTruncated`, and a close between messages
// gives `event: None` and no error, which is an ordinary end of
// connection.
pub fn finish(r: H1Reader) -> Result<H1Step, H1DecodeError>
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.finish")

// pending_len is how many bytes the reader is holding.
//
// For a caller that bounds its own buffering, and for a supervisor that
// wants to notice a peer sitting on half a message forever.  The
// limits bound this too, but a caller may want to act before they do.
pub fn pending_len(r: H1Reader) -> Int
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.pending_len")

// offset is how many bytes this reader has consumed since it was made.
//
// The same counter every `H1DecodeError`'s `at` is measured in, so a
// caller can report a fault's position on a connection without keeping
// a count of its own.
pub fn offset(r: H1Reader) -> Int
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.offset")

// message_open says whether a message is in progress — a head has
// arrived and its `MessageDone` has not.
//
// A caller closing a connection between messages is doing something
// ordinary; a caller closing one mid-message has to say so to the other
// end, which is the difference this answers.
pub fn message_open(r: H1Reader) -> Bool
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.message_open")

// drain reads from `src` until one message ends or the source stops
// giving, and returns every event that came out.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING SIGNATURE OF THIS PACKAGE.  `S: Read[e]` binds the
// effect parameter of the standard library's `Read` trait and the
// clause is `[e]`, so the row means "whatever the impl behind `S`
// supplies": `[io, net]` against a `TcpStream`, nothing against an
// in-memory `Buffer`.  Naming `[e]` has not spent an effect — it has
// said "whatever you hand me" — which is what lets a `core` package
// offer the read-until-done loop instead of making every caller write
// it (SPEC § 5.6, and `docs/publishing.md` § Design calls this the
// direct shape for a core package taking a stream from its host).
//
// It is the same machine as `feed` and `take` with the pump written
// once, here, instead of once per caller.  Use `feed` when the bytes
// arrive from somewhere this does not fit — a TLS record layer, a
// decompressor, a browser's fetch — and use this otherwise.
//
// It is the ONLY function in the package with a row, and the only one
// that could have had one: everything else takes bytes the caller
// already holds.
pub fn drain<S: Read[e]>(r: H1Reader, src: S) -> Result<H1Drained, H1DecodeError> [e]
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.drain")

// parse_head reads one complete head out of a byte list, for a caller
// that already has the whole thing.
//
// The shape `std.http`'s `http_server_parse_head` has, with two
// differences that are the reason this package exists: it takes bytes
// rather than a `Str`, so a head with an invalid UTF-8 byte in it is
// refused rather than mangled on the way in; and it returns a reason
// with an offset rather than `None`.
//
// It reads the HEAD only.  Whatever follows the blank line is the body,
// and framing it is `h1msg.request_framing`'s job — which is why this
// returns the byte count it consumed alongside the head.
pub fn parse_head(bytes: [u8], role: H1Role, limits: H1Limits) -> Result<H1Step, H1DecodeError>
    todo("http-codec-nv.h1read.parse_head")
